WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.882
Shell scripts often make decisions based
on variables, test expressions,

2
00:00:06.962 --> 00:00:11.521
command substitutions, pipelines, and exit
status.

3
00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:16.768
Small mistakes in those areas can cause a
script to skip work,

4
00:00:16.848 --> 00:00:21.508
continue after failure, report success
incorrectly,

5
00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:25.539
or take a branch that the author did not
expect.

6
00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:31.900
ShellCheck can help identify suspicious
variable use, confusing tests,

7
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:39.173
unreachable logic, and places where
command results may not be handled as intended.

8
00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:44.622
Variable findings are especially useful in
maintenance work.

9
00:00:44.702 --> 00:00:49.464
A misspelled variable name may expand to
an empty value.

10
00:00:49.544 --> 00:00:55.014
An unused assignment may indicate dead
code or a forgotten step.

11
00:00:55.094 --> 00:01:01.578
A variable supplied by the environment may
need an explicit presence check.

12
00:01:01.658 --> 00:01:05.812
ShellCheck cannot decide what value is
correct,

13
00:01:05.892 --> 00:01:12.983
but it can draw attention to places where
the script depends on assumptions that are

14
00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:14.583
easy to overlook.

15
00:01:14.663 --> 00:01:19.424
Conditions and exit status deserve
deliberate design.

16
00:01:19.504 --> 00:01:26.495
A command that fails inside a substitution
or pipeline may not produce the failure

17
00:01:26.575 --> 00:01:29.107
behavior the author expects.

18
00:01:29.187 --> 00:01:35.266
A test expression may be syntactically
valid but logically confusing.

19
00:01:35.346 --> 00:01:42.539
A script may check the wrong command's
result or continue after a partial operation.

20
00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:48.089
Reviewers should ask what safe failure
means for this workflow:

21
00:01:48.169 --> 00:01:52.526
stop immediately, retry, skip a
noncritical step,

22
00:01:52.606 --> 00:01:56.354
or report a clear error for human
follow-up.

23
00:01:56.434 --> 00:02:03.019
ShellCheck is a helpful signal, but the
script owner still owns control flow.

24
00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:07.253
Good scripts make important decisions
visible.

25
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:12.398
They check preconditions, handle expected
failure modes,

26
00:02:12.478 --> 00:02:17.341
report meaningful errors, and avoid
ambiguous success.

27
00:02:17.421 --> 00:02:23.803
When ShellCheck points at a variable,
condition, or exit-status pattern,

28
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:30.266
use it as an opportunity to make the
script easier for the next maintainer to

29
00:02:30.346 --> 00:02:31.460
understand.
